// @test: thread boundary accepts a boxed mutable capture
// @description: #384 checkpoint (b)(1) makes the mutable-capture refusal
//   conditional: a closure handed to another thread is escaping by definition, so its
//   mutable captures were boxed instead of holding the spawner frame's address, and
//   the hand-off is sound. Checkpoint (c) then promotes the captured binding to ONE
//   refcounted cell shared by the spawner frame and the spawned closure's environment,
//   so the write the other thread makes through the closure IS visible to the spawner
//   once it joins (`spawner=1`). That is a deliberate semantics change: before (c) the
//   spawned closure wrote into a private cell and the spawner kept its own value
//   (`spawner=0`). Whichever side outlives the other drops its reference, and the last
//   owner runs the cell's destructor.
// @category: threads, thread-boundary, ownership, lambda, closure, mutable
// @expect: pass
// @expect-output: joined=1 spawner=1
// @expect-return: 0
// @env: VYB_STDLIB=stdlib

import threads::{thread_spawn, thread_join}

main()<Int> -> {
    n<Int> = 0
    th<Int> = thread_spawn(|| -> {
        n = n + 1
        return n
    }) else -1
    if (th < 0) { return 1 }

    got<Int> = thread_join(th) else -2
    if (got != 1) { return 2 }

    println("joined=" + got.to_string() + " spawner=" + n.to_string())
    return 0
}
